---
title: "Near-miss references"
bibliography: refs.bib
---

This document deliberately misspells a cross-reference and a citation key, so the
did-you-mean suggestions stay pinned. It lives in `corpus/diagnostics/`, which is
exempt from the corpus clean-render guards.

## Results {#sec-results}

![A fitted curve](results.png){#fig-results}

## Summary {#sec-summary}

A reference that resolves is left alone, and never warns: see @fig-results.

A near-miss figure reference suggests the anchor the author meant: see @fig-reslts.

A near-miss section reference does too: see @sec-summry.

A reference no anchor is near keeps the plain message: see @fig-nonexistent.

A near-miss citation suggests the entry the author meant [@bishop2006patern].

A citation key nothing in the bibliography resembles keeps the plain message
[@nosuchkey].
